'''''Bartramia aprica''''', commonly called '''rigid apple moss''' or sometimes '''upright apple moss''', is a species of moss in the family [[Bartramiaceae]].<ref name=BC_Cons>{{cite web|url=https://a100.gov.bc.ca/pub/eswp/speciesSummary.do;jsessionid=XvcJchJCGDFlXTgMhFt8y31kYV2QDRGpl87wGXPjPG8zbzG1JCjf!-758728675?id=16472|title=Species Summary: Bartramia aprica|publisher=BC Conservation Data Centre|accessdate=21 January 2025}}</ref>
It was formerly known as '''''Bartramia stricta''.'''<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Canada |first=Environment and Climate Change |date=2010-11-24 |title=Rigid apple moss (Bartramia stricta): COSEWIC assessment and statusReport 2009 |url=https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/species-risk-public-registry/cosewic-assessments-status-reports/rigid-apple-moss-2009.html |access-date=2023-09-13 |website=www.canada.ca}}</ref>

It has been observed in California, Texas and New Mexico, USA and British Columbia, Canada.<ref>{{Cite web |title=NatureServe Explorer 2.0 |url=https://explorer.natureserve.org/Taxon/ELEMENT_GLOBAL.2.125208/Bartramia_stricta |access-date=2023-09-13 |website=explorer.natureserve.org}}</ref> It is described as a green to brownish-green perennial moss, growing small erect tufts in scattered colonies across rocky outcrops and shallow soil grasslands.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Canada |first=Environment and Climate Change |date=2010-11-24 |title=Rigid apple moss (Bartramia stricta): COSEWIC assessment and statusReport 2009 |url=https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/species-risk-public-registry/cosewic-assessments-status-reports/rigid-apple-moss-2009.html |access-date=2023-09-13 |website=www.canada.ca}}</ref> Reproduction and dispersal occurs via spores, and as B. stricta has both female and male reproductive structures found within the same plant, ensuring successful fertilisation and sporangia and spore production and dispersal.<ref name=":0" />

